2x - 3 & \text{if $x \ge 4$}
\end{array}
\right.\]
For $f(x)$ to have an inverse, $f(x)$ must be one-to-one.
Let $x < 4.$ Then $f(x) = x + k.$ As $x$ increases, $f(x)$ increases, so $f(x)$ is increasing for $x < 4.$
Let $x \ge 4.$ Then $f(x) = 2x - 3.$ As $x$ increases, $f(x)$ increases, so $f(x)$ is increasing for $x \ge 4.$
We must have $f(x)$ one-to-one.
We have $f(4) = 2(4) - 3 = 8 - 3 = 5.$
