\begin{align*} 997 &= 9 \cdot 100 + 97 \\ 100 &= 1 \cdot 97 + 3 \\ 97 &= 34 \cdot 3 + 1 \\ 3 &= 3 \cdot 1 + 0 \end{align*}
From the Euclidean algorithm, we can backtrack to express 1 as a combination of 100 and 997:

\begin{align*}
1 &= 97 - 34 \cdot 3 \\
&= 97 - 34(100 - 97) \\
&= 35 \cdot 97 - 34 \cdot 100 \\
&= 35(997 - 9 \cdot 100) - 34 \cdot 100 \\
&= 35 \cdot 997 - 339 \cdot 100 - 34 \cdot 100 \\
&= 35 \cdot 997 - 373 \cdot 100
\end{align*}

Thus, $-373 \cdot 100 \equiv 1 \pmod{997}$. Therefore, the multiplicative inverse of $100$ modulo $997$ is $-373$. To find the unique three-digit positive integer, we need to convert $-373$ to a positive equivalent modulo $997$:

\begin{align*}
-373 &\equiv 997 - 373 \pmod{997} \\
&\equiv 624 \pmod{997}
\end{align*}

Therefore, the unique three-digit positive integer $x$ that satisfies the given congruence is $\boxed{624}$.

